r F5 formed a row facing Black's D6 and E6."}, {"turn": 12, "text": "White had been 6.9 points ahead, but C4 lost 10.3 and handed the lead back to Black at +3.4. The engine's choice was H6, away from the left side where Black's B4 stone sat."}, {"turn": 13, "text": "The 20k answered with C5, which lost 11.9 points, its worst move of the game. The lead swung from +3.4 to -8.6, so White was ahead again by a wide margin."}, {"turn": 14, "text": "This was the decisive move. D4 lost 15.7 points, the worst of the game, and moved the lead from -8.6 to +7.1. The engine preferred H5. White had been well ahead, and from here Black never fell behind."}, {"turn": 30, "text": "C2 lost 7.0 points, taking the lead from +15.4 to +22.4, close to the final margin. White was filling in the lower left, and the engine would have played J5 instead."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "The only analysis code is the `inspect` function (also saved as game-180-position-inspector.js), which parses the board, lists chains, liberties and empty regions, and applies a single move the player already named to report captures. The other scripts only called the game tools, replayed the finished move list to verify the final board, and wrote notes and SGF files. Nothing looped over candidate moves, ranked them, or searched ahead.", "stop": false}}